description:
Compare two snapshots of the same block of memory, taken before and after an action (lesson 3.05), and report the 4-byte values that changed, read both as integers and as floats:

```cpp
struct Changed
{
    std::uint64_t address;
    std::int32_t beforeInt, afterInt;
    float beforeFloat, afterFloat;
};

std::vector<Changed> changedDwords(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& before, const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& after, std::uint64_t base);
std::vector<Changed> withoutNoise(const std::vector<Changed>& changes, const std::vector<Changed>& noise);
bool looksLikeFloat(float value);
```

- **changedDwords** compares the two snapshots (the same size) 4 bytes at a time, at offsets 0, 4, 8, ...; every group where any byte differs is reported with its address (`base + offset`) and its values before and after, read little-endian as `std::int32_t` and as `float`. A trailing group of fewer than 4 bytes is ignored.
- **withoutNoise** removes every change whose address also appears in `noise` (changes seen in a snapshot pair taken while doing nothing), keeping the order.
- **looksLikeFloat** is a plausibility check: true for 0 and for finite values whose absolute value is between 0.0001 and 1 000 000. Tiny or enormous values usually mean the bytes are not really a float.

